================================================================================
TEST T44989 -- ACL: Named Hardware on port - send-to-cpu IP
================================================================================
Run:      2026-10-09 (times NZ, box local)
Bench:    tb277, consoles u2, u3, u5; Test Engineer bidhanc@bidhanc-dl
Tester:   bench-runner
Graded:   tester
Case:     AWP-8654, "ACL: Named Hardware on port - send-to-cpu IP", baseline plus
          3 steps
Build:    swi_c  sin_x96056-20260921-9 (netboots), Marvell 98CX8514 Falcon
          swi_b  sin_x96056-20260921-9 (AT-x960-28YSQ, console u3)
Configs:  swi_c.cfg, swi_b.cfg (running-config after setup)

VERDICT: PASS -- a named hardware ACL with the send-to-cpu action, bound to a port,
         consumes the matching frames: they are matched by the rule and never reach
         the far side.
      rule matched the intended flow .. yes        (show access-list counters: 8 hits)
      NOT forwarded to the far side ... correct    (100% packet loss on those 8 pings)
      non-matching source ............. unaffected (0% loss from .66, same port)
      CPU receive-queue 0 ............. does not move (0 -> 0)
      spanning tree across the step ... unchanged  (CR30477 did not reproduce)
      distinct from copy-to-cpu ....... yes        (same hits, opposite forwarding
                                                    outcome, different CPU queue)
CAVEAT:  The CPU half of the action is proven negatively plus by the rule's own hit
         counter: the frames were matched (8 hits) and did not emerge at the far side.
         The frames the CPU received were not inspected, and receive-queue 6 could not
         be used as evidence on this bench (see the method note in STEP 1), so this run
         does not identify which CPU queue send-to-cpu uses -- only that it is not the
         queue copy-to-cpu uses. `access-list hardware-deny-log` was deliberately left
         off throughout, so its documented interaction with a CPU-action ACL was not
         exercised. The traffic was ICMP echo matched by an `ip` rule from one source
         to one destination, ingress on port1.0.48 only.

