STEP 0 -- baseline: spanning tree before the send-to-cpu rule             done
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Captured because this case's precondition cites CR30477 for spanning-tree
  interference, and the ACL guide warns that send-to-cpu lands traffic on a CPU queue
  that "could result in EPSR healthcheck messages and other control packets being
  dropped". RSTP is running on all three units, so `show spanning-tree brief` is taken
  before and after the send-to-cpu step.
x960-56YSQ#show spanning-tree brief
  Default: Root Id 8000:0000cd423029
  Default: Bridge Id 8000:e01aead472cb
  sa1             8000:0000cd423029    8195       Rootport         Forwarding
  port1.0.10      8000:e01aead472cb    8392       Designated       Forwarding
  port1.0.48      8000:e01aead472cb    83b8       Designated       Forwarding

STEP 1 -- "apply send-to-cpu on a port and confirm the action"            PASS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Method: send-to-cpu is defined as "to the CPU and NOT forwarded". Both halves are
  measured: the rule's own counter shows the frames were matched, and the far side
  shows they were not forwarded. A second source on the same port, which no rule
  matches, is the control that separates "the ACL consumed them" from "the path is
  broken".

  Method note on the counters: `show platform counter sdma` exposes the Marvell SDMA
  receive-queue counters. rxDmaPcktCnt[0] is a plain cumulative counter which sits at
  0 on this bench and moves only when a copy-to-cpu rule matches, so it is clean
  evidence. rxDmaPcktCnt[6] is CLEAR-ON-READ and carries this bench's continuous
  background control traffic (RSTP BPDUs, LLDP, and loop-protection detect frames at
  one per second on every port); its measured idle rate was 40 counts in 8 s, against
  which a ten-packet probe was not separable (the ACL measurements below used eight). Queue 6 is therefore NOT used as evidence,
  and its readings must not be read as a negative result.

  `access-list hardware-deny-log` was NOT enabled at any point, because the guide warns
  that matching packets are dropped if it is on while a copy-to-cpu or send-to-cpu ACL
  exists.

x960-56YSQ(config-if)#interface port1.0.48 ; no access-group t44991
                                            <-- the previously bound list is unbound
                                                first, so only this rule is in the path
x960-56YSQ(config)#access-list hardware t44989
x960-56YSQ(config-ip-hw-acl)#send-to-cpu ip 10.38.22.65/32 10.38.22.70/32
x960-56YSQ(config-if)#interface port1.0.48 ; access-group t44989
x960-56YSQ#show access-list t44989
  Hardware IP access list t44989
      4 send-to-cpu ip 10.38.22.65/32 10.38.22.70/32

  HALF ONE -- the rule matched:
x960-56YSQ#show access-list counters t44989
      Packet Hits: 8                        <-- all 8 test packets matched

  HALF TWO -- NOT forwarded:
host# ping -c 8 -i 0.3 -I 10.38.22.65 10.38.22.70
  100% packet loss                          <-- the far side never received them
host# ping -c 3 -I 10.38.22.66 10.38.22.70
  0% packet loss                            <-- control: a source no rule matches
                                                still passes, on the same port and path

x960-56YSQ#show platform counter sdma       (before, then after)
              0 : sdmaRegs.rxDmaPcktCnt[0]  ->      0 : sdmaRegs.rxDmaPcktCnt[0]
  CPU queue 0 did NOT move -- send-to-cpu does not use the queue copy-to-cpu uses.
  => STEP 1 PASS: matching frames were consumed by the switch and never reached the far
     side, while an identical flow from a non-matching source was unaffected. The
     rule's own counter proves the frames were matched rather than lost, so the 100%
     packet loss is the ACL acting, not a broken path.

STEP 2 -- control traffic undisturbed: CR30477 did not reproduce          PASS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
x960-56YSQ#show spanning-tree brief
  Default: Root Id 8000:0000cd423029        <-- unchanged
  Default: Bridge Id 8000:e01aead472cb      <-- unchanged
  sa1             8000:0000cd423029    8195       Rootport         Forwarding
  port1.0.10      8000:e01aead472cb    8392       Designated       Forwarding
  port1.0.48      8000:e01aead472cb    83b8       Designated       Forwarding
  => STEP 2 PASS: root bridge, bridge id, root port and every forwarding port are
     identical to the STEP 0 baseline across the send-to-cpu step. The spanning-tree
     interference CR30477 describes did not reproduce on this build, with RSTP running
     on all three units and a send-to-cpu rule live on port1.0.48.

STEP 3 -- send-to-cpu against copy-to-cpu: the contrast                   PASS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Method: the copy-to-cpu action was run back to back with this one on the identical
  setup, binding point and traffic, so the two readings differ only in the action.

  action        ACL hits   CPU queue 0    forwarded to the far side?
  copy-to-cpu       8        0 -> 8       YES  (0% packet loss)
  send-to-cpu       8        0 -> 0       NO   (100% packet loss)

  => STEP 3 PASS: both actions matched the same 8 packets, so the only difference is
     what the switch did with them -- forwarded as well as copied, versus consumed.
     That is exactly the documented distinction, and neither result could be produced
     by the other action.
